{
  "id": "coupe_decale_ivorian",
  "name": "Coupé-décalé (Ivorian dance music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Coupé-décalé Ivorian dance-pop genre emerging in early-2000s Paris among the Ivorian diaspora club scene and rapidly transferred back to Abidjan. The name derives from Nouchi street-slang (\"couper\" to grab money, \"décaler\" to slip away) describing the get-rich-and-flee aspirational lifestyle. Production centers on hard four-on-the-floor digital kick with Nouchi-French lyrical content, simple call-and-response chorus, and electronic synth-bass. The death of founding figure Stéphane Hamidou Doukouré (Douk Saga) in 2006 mythologized the Paris-Abidjan founder-generation.",
  "recipe": "classic 2000-2003 coupé-décalé, electronic Dance, African dance branch, Afro-diasporic MC rhythm south african kwaito kwaito. extended whistle, rap, overtone rich sustained, narrative. roland tr 8s modern aira digital recreation TR-808, sub bass analog synth, ampeg svt amp rock bass amp alder pyramid pure nickel bass electric bass. decca west africa subsidiary 1947-1975 mid tier lagos decca west african studio. tube condenser, 500-series boutique pre, brick-wall limiter, dynamic EQ. 24-bit digital plate reverb digital delay pitch shifter.",
